#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "90308076-a0ef-5e6a-900d-2ef4c8107907" :question/math-academy-id "e-5466" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $\\mathbf{v}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-15-(-20)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "7969785b-fc71-50f4-9d73-da5599630b6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5467" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10),$ and $(-4,-3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n\n- $(4,-3)$\n\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a021e344-a3d3-5d81-8d0d-71a821517f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283855} {:answer/id #uuid "d824e97e-c767-5491-9b27-57197b58eefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d824e97e-c767-5491-9b27-57197b58eefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283856} :answer-field/id #uuid "46cc790d-e372-586c-9565-30757ab035be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2bd5967-0a06-5e5f-90ed-f30f5110edfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2bd5967-0a06-5e5f-90ed-f30f5110edfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283858} :answer-field/id #uuid "42cc3774-b7a2-5d8e-ab2e-f8af50711a34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4055556b-4590-59c0-ae15-eb16a5349d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-317509" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(3)-(5)(1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
